Sofabaton Integration — Setup Guide

This guide walks through setting up a Sofabaton hub with this integration, start to finish. There are two hub models, and they work differently, so find your model below and follow that section. Don't skip the "Before you start" section, both models need it.

🟠 X1S — one-way read (local, instant), commands sent via cloud webhook
🟢 X2 — one-way read (local, instant, via MQTT), commands sent via cloud webhook (local two-way command control is planned but blocked on a platform issue — see Known Issues below)

Throughout this guide, each step's tag sits on its own line right above it, so the step numbers all line up and you can tell at a glance which app the step below it happens in:

🔷 Hubitat — the step below happens in the Sofabaton Integration app or a device page
🟣 Sofabaton app — the step below happens in the Sofabaton mobile app, not Hubitat at all
Before you start (both models)
Give your Sofabaton hub a static IP. Set a DHCP reservation for it on your router. Both setup paths below need an IP address that will never change — if it changes later, the integration breaks silently until you fix it.
Install this integration. In Hubitat: Apps → Add User App → search "Sofabaton Integration". This creates the app and, once you add your first hub, a Sofabaton Integration Bridge device underneath it automatically.
Know which hub you have. It's printed on the hub itself, or check the Sofabaton app under the hub's settings.
🟠 X1S Setup
Step 1 — Add the hub in Hubitat

🔷 Hubitat

In the Sofabaton Integration app: Add a Hub → Hub Model: X1S → enter the hub's static IP address from step 1 above → Add This Hub.

Step 2 — Point the Sofabaton app at Hubitat (for reads)

This is what lets the physical remote tell Hubitat "an activity changed."

🟣 Sofabaton app

Devices → Add Device → Wi-Fi → "Create a virtual device for IP control"
URL: http://[your Hubitat IP]:39501/
Method: PUT
Body: this is the part that's confusing at first, see What do button 11-20 actually mean? below before picking a value.
Repeat this for every activity you want Hubitat to know about.
Step 3 — Turn on the cloud API (for commands)

This is what lets Hubitat actually turn an activity on/off. It's a separate step from Step 2, don't skip it thinking Step 2 covers both directions, it doesn't.

🟣 Sofabaton app

Find the activity → look for "Turn on API" (this is sometimes just called "API" depending on app version)
Enable it. The app will generate a webhook URL — a long https://app1.sofabaton.com/... link. It may generate a separate URL for turning the activity on and off, or just one depending on the app version.
Copy this URL down. You'll paste it into Hubitat in the next step.

⚠️ If the URL you copy contains a space (e.g. ...&id=Watch Apple TV&type=0), that's normal, the driver automatically encodes it — you don't need to edit it yourself.

Step 4 — Add the Activity in Hubitat

🔷 Hubitat

In the Sofabaton Integration app: Add an Activity → pick this hub →

Activity Name: must exactly match the label you're using for this activity on the physical remote. See How does name matching work? below, this trips people up.
Start Activity Webhook URL: paste the URL from Step 3
Stop Activity Webhook URL: paste the "off" URL from Step 3, if your app version gave you a separate one. If it didn't, leave this blank.

Click Add This Activity.

Step 5 — Finish

🔷 Hubitat

Back on the main app page, click Done at the bottom. This step is easy to miss, and skipping it means the app never fully installs — hubs and activities you already added will still work, but you should still click Done once you're finished for the session.

Worked example — an "Apple TV" activity

To make all of the above concrete, here's the same activity carried through every step:

Where	What you enter	Value
Step 2, body value	the text you send from the Sofabaton app	appleTV
Remote device Preferences, User 1 (11)	matchString|Description	appleTV|Apple TV
Step 4, Activity Name in Hubitat	must match the Description half above	Apple TV

appleTV (the matchString) has to exactly equal what you typed as the body value in Step 2. Apple TV (the Description, and the Hubitat Activity Name) has to exactly equal each other, capitalization and spacing included. If any of these three don't line up, the remote still controls your gear fine, Hubitat just won't know the activity changed.

🟢 X2 Setup
Step 1 — Get the built-in MQTT broker running

🔷 Hubitat

Integrations → Add Built-In App → MQTT Import Integration
Enable it, turn on "Use built-in MQTT service"
Note the host, port, and login (username/password) shown — you'll need these in Step 2 and Step 4.
Step 2 — Connect the physical hub to that broker

This is the step that's easy to miss, and without it, the X2 hub never talks to the broker at all, no matter how correctly everything else is set up.

🟣 Sofabaton app

Go to Me → Connect to Home Assistant (MQTT broker) — this is not the same thing as "Devices → Add Device → Wi-Fi → Home Assistant Remote", that's a different, one-way trigger feature and doesn't connect the hub to anything.
Enter the same host/port from Step 1
When it asks for "Home Assistant account credentials," enter the broker username/password from Step 1, that's all it actually wants
Confirm it shows connected/running
Step 3 — Find your X2 hub's MAC address

This is not your Hubitat hub's MAC, it's the Sofabaton hub's own MAC, and it's easy to grab the wrong one.

🟣 Sofabaton hub (via an external MQTT tool)

Connect an MQTT client (like MQTT Explorer) to the broker from Step 1, using the host/port/login it gave you
Press any activity button on the physical Sofabaton remote
Look for a topic like activity/14639332AA40/activity_control_up — the 12-character hex string in the middle is your X2's MAC
Step 4 — Add the hub in Hubitat

🔷 Hubitat

In the Sofabaton Integration app: Add a Hub → Hub Model: X2 →

Sofabaton Hub MAC ID: the MAC from Step 3
Hubitat Hub's LAN IP: the broker host from Step 1 (this is Hubitat's IP, not the Sofabaton hub's IP)
Broker Port / Username / Password: from Step 1

Click Add This Hub.

Step 5 — Turn on the cloud API (for commands)

🟣 Sofabaton app

Same as X1S Step 3 above: find the activity, turn on "Turn on API", copy the webhook URL(s) it generates.

Step 6 — Add the Activity in Hubitat

🔷 Hubitat

In the Sofabaton Integration app: Add an Activity → pick this hub →

Activity Name: any name you want (X2 doesn't require name-matching the way X1S does — X2 matches by numeric ID instead, see next field)
Sofabaton Activity ID: this is required, it's what lets Hubitat know which physical activity this is when the remote reports a change. Either:
Click Listen for Next Activity, then press the activity's button on the physical remote, then come back to this page (it doesn't auto-refresh) and the ID will be filled in, or
Type it in manually if you already know it (readable from the activity_control_up MQTT topic payload, same way you found the MAC)
Start / Stop Activity Webhook URL (optional but recommended right now, see Known Issues): paste the URL(s) from Step 5

Click Add This Activity.

Step 7 — Finish

🔷 Hubitat

Same as X1S: click Done at the bottom of the main app page.

Worked example — the same "Apple TV" activity
Where	What you enter	Value
Step 3/6, MAC / topic	read off the MQTT payload	14639332AA40
Step 6, Sofabaton Activity ID	a plain number, e.g. {"activity_id":101,"state":"on"}	101
Step 6, Activity Name in Hubitat	just a label, doesn't need to match anything	Apple TV

Unlike X1S, the Activity Name here is purely cosmetic, it never has to match anything in the Sofabaton app. The Sofabaton Activity ID is the only value that actually has to be correct.

Known issues right now

X2 command delivery over MQTT doesn't currently work. MQTT is a fully two-way protocol by design, publish and subscribe both work in either direction, that's the whole point of it, and it's why this integration was built around MQTT for X2 in the first place. Reads (remote → Hubitat) work fine over MQTT right now. Writes (Hubitat → hub) don't, and that's specifically because Hubitat's own MQTT client isn't delivering incoming messages properly on this platform, a reported platform-level bug, not a limitation of MQTT itself or of the X2 hub. Until it's fixed, fill in the webhook URL fields when adding an X2 Activity — that gives you working on/off control today, over the cloud, while MQTT command delivery is unavailable. Once the platform bug is fixed, no changes will be needed on your end, both directions will work over MQTT as originally intended.

FAQ / things that aren't obvious
What do buttons 11-20 actually mean? (X1S)

The X1S's local HTTP path has two separate button systems:

Buttons 1-10 ("Numeric"): if the value sent is a plain number (1-10), Hubitat fires that exact button number. Simple, but capped at 10.
Buttons 11-20 ("User Definable"): each of the 10 slots holds a matchString|Description pair you configure on the Remote device (Preferences tab). The matchString is whatever value you choose to send from Step 2 (any text, not just a number), and when Hubitat receives that exact text, it fires the corresponding button (11-20). The Description is the friendly label — and it's also what name matching (below) uses.

For most setups, ignore the numeric 1-10 system entirely and use the string-based 11-20 slots, it's more flexible and self-documenting (e.g. watchAppleTV instead of remembering that "7" means Apple TV).

How does name matching work? (X1S)

For X1S, Hubitat learns an activity changed by matching the Description you set on a Remote device's button slot (see above) against the Activity Name you typed when adding the Activity in the app. These two must be spelled exactly the same, including capitalization and spacing. If they don't match, the physical remote will still control your gear, but Hubitat won't know the state changed.

Where exactly do I put the webhook URL?

Into the Activity's Start/Stop Activity Webhook URL fields, either when you first add the Activity in the app, or afterward via that Activity's Edit button on the main page.

My activity has a space in the name — will the webhook break?

No. The driver automatically URL-encodes spaces before making the call, you can paste the webhook URL exactly as the Sofabaton app gives it to you.

Do I need MQTT for X1S?

No. X1S never uses MQTT at all, only local HTTP (read) and the cloud webhook (write).

Can X2 use the same local HTTP read path as X1S instead of MQTT?

Not currently, this integration's X2 support is built around MQTT identification (by MAC). Using X1S's local HTTP path for an X2 hub would require re-adding it as hub model "X1S" instead, which uses IP-based identification and a different state-sync method (name matching, not numeric ID). This works, but is a manual reconfiguration, not something this app does automatically.

Getting help

If something in this guide doesn't match what you're seeing, or a step doesn't work as described, please open an issue on the GitHub repo rather than guessing, hub firmware and app versions do change behavior over time.
